{
 "id": "imperfect-passive",
 "title": "Imperfect Passive Indicative",
 "course": "Verbs · Passive Indicative",
 "intro": "The passive uses the same stems and time markers as the active. Only the personal endings change: <span class=\"end\">-r, -ris, -tur, -mur, -minī, -ntur</span> replace <span class=\"end\">-ō/-m, -s, -t, -mus, -tis, -nt</span>.<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\">The imperfect keeps its linking vowel + <span class=\"end\">-bā-</span>, exactly as in the active: amā-, vidē-, mittē-, capiē-, audiē-. So amābam → amābar, amābant → amābantur.</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\">The ending -bāris has an alternate <b>-bāre</b> (amābāre), which is not drilled here.</p>",
 "paradigmNote": "Passive endings: <span class=\"end\">-r, -ris, -tur, -mur, -minī, -ntur</span>",
